{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>He was a mighty hunter before YHWH.</b> He would hunt the minds of creatures with his mouth and persuade them to rebel against the Authority. \"Therefore it is said\" of any person who sins with brazenness and knows his Master yet intentionally rebels against Him — it is said of him: \"like Nimrod.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 10:9), and likewise the view of our Sages (Eruvin 53a). And Rabbi Abraham explained the opposite of this matter according to the plain sense: that he began to be mighty over the animals, to hunt them, and he explained \"before YHWH\" to mean that he would build altars and offer the animals as a burnt offering before HaShem. But his words do not appear correct, for he is thereby justifying a wicked man, whereas our Sages knew his wickedness by tradition. And the correct view in my eyes is that he began to rule over people by his might, and he was the first king, for until his days there were no wars and no king had reigned. He first prevailed over the people of Babylon until he became king over them, and afterward he went forth to Assyria, and did as he wished, and grew great, and built there fortified cities through his power and might. And this is what it says: \"And the beginning of his kingdom was Babylon and Erech and Accad and Calneh\" (Genesis 10:10)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:04.188864271Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}